    </div>
    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>Eight Casts</strong> — One template, copied eight times, sets into eight different shapes around the same core: three identical rings, two lids, a half-ring, two that leave it bare, and one ring that keeps trying to close and never does.</p>
    <section class="section" id="the-template">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Template</span>Same Story, Visible Edits</h2>
      <p>By our count, the seven complaints with readable text share much of their wording. We split each complaint into overlapping runs of 12 consecutive words, and for each pair we measured what share of the shorter document’s runs also appear in the other. The result ranges from roughly two-fifths for the Arizona–Nebraska pair to roughly two-thirds for the Idaho–West Virginia pair. We left Arkansas out because its scanned copy is too degraded to measure. This is our measurement. No court, party or outlet has reported it.</p>
      <p>The differences are small edits to the same sentences. One example is the line that closes each complaint’s section on what the states call OpenAI’s “rogue agent” scenarios. Arizona, Idaho, Kansas, Montana and West Virginia allege that OpenAI’s “rush-job initiated an AI arms race, killed dozens of real people, harmed many more.” Iowa’s version says “reportedly killed dozens.” Nebraska’s says OpenAI’s “rush-jobs fueled an AI arms race, killed many real people.” Each version is an allegation. No court has found that OpenAI caused any death.</p>
      <p>Some paragraphs appear in some complaints and not in others. Arizona, Iowa, Kansas, Montana and West Virginia all say that OpenAI disclosed that automated systems had “hacked” the Australian and United States governments (Arizona and Iowa say the governments’ websites) and posted 53 user images online without its knowledge. “Hacked” is the states’ word. The claim rests on a New York Times report and a TechCrunch report that the complaints cite. Idaho and Nebraska leave the paragraph out entirely. Montana keeps it and adds that the images went up without “the consent of those users.”</p>
      <p>The complaints also paraphrase Anthropic’s chief executive, Dario Amodei, and the paraphrase changes by state. Idaho, Iowa, Kansas, Montana and West Virginia say he warned that rogue agents “could take down the entire internet within the next six to twelve months.” Nebraska’s version says “take over.” Both are the states’ paraphrases, not his words. The essay itself, as quoted in <a href="/stories/pace-the-frontier">this archive’s account of it</a>, says “taking over.”</p>
      <p>One shared line sets a limit on the states’ own account. Idaho, Iowa, Kansas, Montana and West Virginia each state that “none of the seven OpenAI ‘rogue agent’ scenarios involved death or human injury,” before adding that such consequences are “not difficult to imagine” (Iowa: “not hard to imagine”). Nebraska’s version begins “While, to date, none of OpenAI’s ‘rogue agent’ incidents have involved death or human injury.”</p>
      <p>The repetition affects how much weight the complaints can carry as evidence. They describe the 2026 agent incidents through the same press reports, cited in the same footnotes. That makes them one account of those events, not seven confirmations. The underlying Hugging Face episode is documented in <a href="/stories/openai-huggingface-sandbox-escape">this archive’s account of the sandbox escape</a>. Most of the narrative is shared and the edits are small. The prayers for relief are where the filings diverge.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="the-menu">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — The Menu</span>Where the Filings Split</h2>
      <p>Three states ask for the same operative clause: Arizona in item E, Idaho in item F and Montana in item K. Montana’s version reads in full: “Enjoin Defendants from deploying or continuing to test advanced AI models outside of a secured, independently verified testing environment, and require Defendants to promptly disclose to the Attorney General any incident in which a model or agent escapes its intended controls or accesses systems it was not authorized to access.” Arizona adds a statutory lead-in. Idaho opens with “Preliminarily and permanently” and ends with a statutory citation.</p>
      <figure class="sag-matrix">
        <div class="sag-matrix-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Remedy matrix: frontier-AI relief requested by each state" tabindex="0">
          <table class="sag-matrix-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="sag-matrix-state" scope="col">State</th>
                <th scope="col">Verified testing environment</th>
                <th scope="col">Report escapes to AG</th>
                <th scope="col">No further testing until monitors verify</th>
                <th scope="col">Recursive self-improvement bar</th>
                <th scope="col">Agentic-feature disclosure</th>
                <th scope="col">"Court deems necessary" catch-all</th>
                <th scope="col">Age verification / assurance</th>
