      <p>Iowa and Arkansas take a different approach. Iowa’s item I and Arkansas’s item m would bar “any further testing of advanced AI models” until adequate safeguards are in place and third-party monitors have verified them. Arkansas specifies “independent third-party monitors.” Our reading: the Arizona clause lets testing continue inside a verified environment, while the Iowa and Arkansas clause stops testing until someone outside the company signs off. Only Arkansas adds item n, the recursive self-improvement bar quoted at the top of this piece.</p>
      <p>West Virginia’s requests target the product. Item F would bar OpenAI from deploying “agentic or other features” to West Virginia consumers without disclosing material safety incidents. It would also require OpenAI to report escapes. Item G asks for age assurance, crisis-escalation protocols and “an independent monitor reporting to the Attorney General.” It then sets its own limit: the relief “does not require Defendants to alter the viewpoint or subject matter of lawful information provided to adults.” Our reading: that sentence is written to head off a free-speech objection. Item H asks the court to make OpenAI fund a court-supervised abatement plan.</p>
      <figure class="sag-diff">
        <div class="sag-diff-cols">
