              <span class="sag-diff-tag">ID: "Preliminarily and permanently …, I.C. §§ 48-606(1)(b), 607;"</span>
              <span class="sag-diff-tag">MT: (no additions)</span>
            </div>
            <p>Enjoin Defendants from deploying <del>or continuing to test advanced AI models outside of a secured, independently verified testing environment</del>, and require Defendants to <del>promptly disclose</del> to the Attorney General any incident in which a model or agent escapes its intended controls or accesses <del>systems it was not authorized to access</del>;</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sag-diff-col">
            <div class="sag-diff-label">West Virginia F</div>
            <p>Enjoin Defendants from deploying <ins>agentic or other features to West Virginia consumers without disclosure of material safety incidents affecting such features</ins>, and require Defendants to <ins>report</ins> to the Attorney General any incident in which a model or agent <ins>made available to West Virginia consumers</ins> escapes its intended controls or accesses <ins>West Virginia systems without authorization</ins>;</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="sag-diff-caption">The same reporting duty, narrowed to one state's consumers and systems. Struck text is wording West Virginia's version does not use; underlined text appears only in West Virginia's. The comparison does not show which text came first.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Kansas and Nebraska ask for no frontier-testing, containment or court-designed safety relief. Their prayers seek declarations, injunctions against deceptive practices, civil penalties and restitution. Nebraska also asks for compensatory damages. Among the prayers we could read, the requested penalties run from $2,000 to $50,000 per violation, and both figures come from Nebraska’s complaint, under two different statutes.</p>
      <p>Five states include a catch-all: Arizona D, Arkansas o, Idaho G, Iowa J and Montana L. Each asks the court to require “additional safety measures and protocols” that “the Court deems necessary.” In effect, these clauses hand the definition of “safe enough” to the judge.</p>
      <p>These clauses exist only as requests. Each attorney general chose which ones to put before a single county judge.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="the-other-track">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — The Other Track</span>A Release Gate</h2>
      <p>The Texas attorney general’s office has published a petition against OpenAI dated 23 October. Its caption names Collin County, but the cause number is blank and the copy has no clerk stamp. We have not confirmed that it was filed. The petition names six OpenAI entities and Sam Altman personally. Its outside counsel are Ashley Keller, Jessica Beringer and Alex Dravillas of Keller Postman, and Anthony G. Buzbee and Christopher J. Leavitt of The Buzbee Law Firm. Kibler Fowler &amp; Cave does not appear.</p>
      <p>Texas puts its gate at a different point. Item j of paragraph 208 would bar OpenAI from “releasing any new artificial intelligence model to Texas users unless an independent third-party evaluator, approved by the Court, has reviewed its safety guardrails and approved its release.” Our reading: Arizona puts the gate around testing, Iowa and Arkansas put it before testing, and Texas puts it at release. Elsewhere the petition reaches further. Item f would bar “offering ChatGPT to minors in Texas.”</p>
      <p>Florida has made a similar request in its own case against OpenAI. Its 28 September motion for a temporary injunction in Highlands County lists the same three Keller Postman lawyers among its counsel. Heading I.A of the motion argues that OpenAI “should be enjoined from developing new artificial intelligence models without third-party approved safety guardrails.”</p>
      <p>By our count, using the same 12-word method, the Texas petition shares only 3 to 4 percent of its passages with each of the seven measurable complaints. Florida’s motion and the Texas petition share about 15 percent, and Florida’s motion shares under 1 percent with the seven. Our reading is that there are two sets of outside counsel, two bodies of text and two different theories of where the gate belongs.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="signal">
      <h2><span class="num">06 — Signal</span>What a Judge Would Have to Decide</h2>
      <p>As far as we could find, nothing has happened in court since the filings. We found no reported removal, consolidation or hearing. West Virginia’s cover sheet marks all eight defendants “Hold for Later Service” and says the case “will be ready for trial by” 14 April 2028. That date is the filer’s estimate on a form, not a scheduled trial.</p>
      <p>We could not locate a direct statement from OpenAI. A 16 October client alert from the law firm Troutman Pepper Locke relays a statement attributed to an OpenAI spokesperson. According to the alert, the statement points to parental controls and other safeguards. The alert does not say where the statement first appeared. We found no OpenAI response to Texas.</p>
      <p>The requests do not all fit together. Under the Arizona, Idaho and Montana clause, a lab keeps testing inside a verified environment. Under the Iowa and Arkansas clause, it stops testing until monitors sign off. Under Texas’s request, a court-approved evaluator stands between the lab and every new release to Texas users. West Virginia’s reporting duty stops at the state line, and Kansas and Nebraska ask for none of this. If any of these clauses is granted, one county judge will have picked one definition, and the order will bind a company whose models do not stop at state borders.</p>
      <p>No judge has granted any of these clauses, and none of these suits has yet placed a testing obligation on OpenAI. What is still open is which gate, if any, a county judge will reach first.</p>
    </section>

